Wait — is there a changelog lint/format check? `scripts/check_changelog_size.sh` only checks size. Also is CHANGELOG.md wrapped at some width? Looking at existing entries, lines wrap around 78 chars. My entry does too. Good.

## Thread 5 — byte-count refactor

Current state in `_persist`:
```dart
final id = store.allocateId('$childId-$idBase');
if (checkEnvelopeFit) {
 final overhead = _statusLine(status, id, redacted.length, redacted).length + 2;
 ...
}
store.put(id, redacted);
final meta = AgentArtifactMeta(..., chars: redacted.length, bytes: utf8.encode(redacted).length, reportId: reportId);
store.recordArtifact(id, meta);
_persistRow(id, redacted, status, childId, reportId);
return DeliverableRecord.artifact(..., chars: redacted.length, bytes: meta.bytes!);
``

And `_statusLine(status, id, chars, stored)` does `utf8.encode(stored).length` twice (per status branch), used in `_persist` (fit check) and `envelopeText`.

And `_runRestore` does `utf8.encode(content).length` once per row.

Refactor: compute `bytes` once at the `_persist` boundary:
```dart
final bytes = utf8.encode(redacted).length;
if (checkEnvelopeFit) {
 final overhead = _statusLine(status, id, redacted.length, bytes).length + 2;
 ...
}
store.put(id, redacted);
final meta = AgentArtifactMeta(..., bytes: bytes, ...);
...
return DeliverableRecord.artifact(..., bytes: bytes);
``

`_statusLine` signature: `(AgentArtifactStatus status, String id, int chars, int bytes)`.

`envelopeText` calls `_statusLine(record.status!, record.outputId!, record.chars, stored)` → uses `record.bytes!`... record.bytes could be null for legacy records — but envelopeText is only called on records produced by _persist (bytes set). To be safe: `record.bytes ?? utf8.encode(stored).length`. Hmm — cleaner: `final bytes = record.bytes ?? utf8.encode(stored).length;` avoids `!`.

Restore: `bytes: utf8.encode(content).length` — single encode already; keep.

That drops the per-spill encodes from 3-4 to 1 (+1 on restore path only).

Also the fit-check comment says "(deterministic, so the fit check can run BEFORE anything is persisted)" — keep semantics.

Existing tests that pin exact envelope text and byte counts will catch mistakes. Run them after.

